## Rules

- **Tokens only.** Every colour comes from a semantic token — `bg-background`,
  `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`. Never a hex value, never a Tailwind
  palette class like `bg-blue-500`. This is the rule that keeps the design
  swappable, and it is the one most often broken when working quickly.
- **Update shadcn primitives, not call sites.** If the project already has local
  shadcn components (`src/components/ui/*.tsx`), copy the kitchen sink's classes
  into that component's own definition — its `cva` variants, its base className —
  not into each place it's used. A design's shape (radius, border weight, shadow)
  is a property of the component, not of any one screen that happens to use it.
- **Verify with a `/kitchensink` page.** After updating the primitives, build a
  `/kitchensink` route rendering the same components, in the same order, as the
  design's own `index.html` — using the project's own updated components, not
  copied markup. Compare the two visually before calling the work done. Leave the
  route in place rather than deleting it — it pays for itself the next time a
  design changes or a primitive drifts.
- **The three axes own different things.** The layout decides structure — regions,
  sizes, responsive behaviour, scroll model. The design decides appearance — colour,
  type, spacing, component styling. The voice decides words. If two of them appear
  to conflict on anything else, ask rather than guessing.
- **Follow the "Never" list** in each of the three files. If a rule genuinely can't
  be followed, say why instead of silently breaking it.
- **Accessibility is a contract, not a polish pass.** WCAG 2.2 AA, per
  `shared/ACCESSIBILITY.md`. Real `<label>`s, `aria-label` on icon-only buttons, a
  visible focus ring on everything, and state never carried by colour alone.
- **Both light and dark** are part of the deliverable, not an afterthought.
- **Copy is part of the deliverable too.** Buttons, empty states, errors and
  confirmations all get written in the named voice — not filled with placeholder
  text to be cleaned up later.

## Implementation checklist (read before touching `src/components/ui/**`)

The rules above describe what "on-design" means. These describe how components
actually go wrong in practice — each one below has been found and fixed after
the fact on a real first pass.

- **shadcn's registry defaults are a starting point, not the target.** A pulled
  component will compile and look plausible while being off-spec in ways that
  are easy to miss at a glance — control height, radius, border weight,
  shadow, and stray non-token colours the registry ships with (e.g.
  `text-white` on a destructive variant instead of `text-destructive-foreground`).
  **Before wiring up any primitive, open the matching kitchen-sink section**
  (`designs/{{DESIGN}}/index.html`, the section whose `id` matches — `#buttons`,
  `#card`, `#badges`, …) **and diff the pulled component's classes against it
  line by line.** Don't assume "it's shadcn, it must already match."
- **The kitchen sink demo outranks this file's own prose when they disagree.**
  The demo is a rendered answer, not a description of one — treat that
  literally. If `designs/{{DESIGN}}/DESIGN.md`'s prose says one thing and the
  rendered kitchen sink (or `theme.css`) does another, copy the demo and flag
  the mismatch rather than guessing which one is authoritative.
- **After styling a primitive, verify the rendered output, not just the
  classes.** Screenshot the page in both themes — the `/kitchensink` page above
  is what this is for. For anything that supports `asChild`, specifically
  check an instance rendered through it (e.g. a
  `<Button asChild><a>…</a></Button>` navigation link, per the "never a
  button styled as a link if it navigates" rule in `shared/ACCESSIBILITY.md`)
  — that's the case most likely to silently drop styling a plain instance
  would have. A class list that "looks right" is not the same as a computed
  style that matches the kitchen sink.
- **Radix's `asChild` can leak button-only attributes onto whatever it wraps.**
  `Dialog.Close` / `Trigger` and similar Radix primitives default to injecting
  `type="button"` (and forwarding `data-slot`) onto their child via `asChild` —
  including a plain `<a>`, if that's what's inside. Check the rendered
  attributes (DOM inspection or `getComputedStyle`, not just the `className`
  list) on anything wrapped in a Radix `asChild`. Prefer a controlled
  `open`/`onOpenChange` state with an `onClick` handler over `Dialog.Close
  asChild` when the thing being closed is a navigation link rather than an
  actual button — it sidesteps the attribute injection entirely instead of
  fighting it.
